00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:59.500
to be split then I can update my inner node because I still hold the lock to it.

458
00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:01.740
So that's okay, we can do this.

459
00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:05.120
But what happens if my inner node is full too?

460
00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.240
So then I would have to go one step further.

461
00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:12.140
So this of course we cannot do, because if you start doing log coupling in the wrong

462
00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:13.260
direction then we get that looks.

463
00:33:15.340 --> 00:33:17.940
Now there are multiple ways to do this.

464
00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:24.640
One way to solve this problem is so called safe inner pages, which means basically when

465
00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:27.720
we go down we check if my inner page is full.

466
00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:34.800
and if yes we split it even though in this moment we don't need all the space

467
00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:41.440
so we see my inner page is full and then I split it and because I did the same

468
00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:46.460
with my parent page of course this means I never have to propagate further

469
00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:52.340
than one level and I already hold the level as one level so when we go down we go to the root

470
00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:57.580
If my root page is full, then we split the root page, create a new root.

471
00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:02.120
Otherwise I go to this page and check if the page is full.

472
00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:06.840
If yes, in this moment in time I still hold the log to my parent, which means I can split

473
00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.600
myself, update the parent and I am done.

474
00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:14.480
Otherwise I release the log to my parent, I go down to the leaf and again the same.

475
00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:21.660
So we check inner pages in the moment they are full, even though we don't need it, but

476
00:34:21.660 --> 00:34:26.980
not strictly needed, but this guarantees I said a proper split never propagates higher

477
00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:29.100
up than one level and this is safe with low coupling.

478
00:34:30.700 --> 00:34:35.920
Now downside of course is we waste one element on the inner page because we could have waited

479
00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:36.780
one insert longer.

480
00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:38.400
So we do it one insert too early.

481
00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:43.780
But that's okay because I mean if I can fit 1000 elements on the inner page, if I do this

482
00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:46.920
Too early means this is not a critical issue.

483
00:34:47.280 --> 00:34:52.280
So I'm wasting one promilio of my space, that's okay.

484
00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:57.420
That's a very small overhead and the logic is pretty simple.

485
00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:02.660
So I don't have all this nasty, complicated, going up logic.

486
00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:03.580
No, this is good.

487
00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:14.740
Now, the main problem with save pages, I mean why don't we always use save pages, the main

488
00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:16.600
problem with save pages are strings.

489
00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:23.380
Because if you have string values, it's not so easy to decide if your page is full.

490
00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:27.040
So how do I know if I have enough space for one more element?

491
00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:37.240
And so for integer values, that's not a problem because I know I can just need 4 bytes or

492
00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:38.720
8 bytes, it depends on my data type.

493
00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:40.440
I know when I can squeeze this in.

494
00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:43.340
But if I have string columns, I don't know this.

495
00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:52.780
And this makes save pages unsuited for variable size records.

496
00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:59.960
So I cannot really decide if I should split this page or not.

497
00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:05.060
So what we can do as an alternative is what is shown here, this is a so called restart

498
00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:05.720
strategy.

499
00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:12.640
So basically what we do is we first try using simple log coupling.

500
00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:15.220
So we just go down and then it.

501
00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:19.120
So if we do not have to split the inner nodes, everything is okay.

502
00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:24.020
We can split the relief node, but we don't split the inner node.

503
00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:28.940
So otherwise, if you do have to split the inner page, then we release all the ledges

504
00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:36.800
and now start again, but during the second walk down, we keep all the ledges up to the

505
00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:37.020
root.

506
00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:41.240
That means that we don't release them, we keep them.

507
00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:47.740
And now we know that all operations can be executed safely, because I have all the ledges

508
00:36:47.740 --> 00:36:48.360
up to the root.

509
00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:54.560
The good thing is this is pretty easy to implement, simple to implement.

510
00:36:57.340 --> 00:37:02.080
Downside of course is if this happens, I mean keep all the latches up to the root,

511
00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:04.720
this is expensive because I really lock my whole path.

512
00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:06.340
So this is a pretty invasive operation.

513
00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:08.440
On the other hand, this happens rarely.

514
00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:10.080
So how do you know this?

515
00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:14.960
I mean if you can fit about 1000 elements on a page, this means if you split a page,

516
00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:21.140
In the inner page, roughly every 1000 inserts, that depends on how you implement it, perhaps

517
00:37:21.140 --> 00:37:23.040
every 500, but roughly every 1000.

518
00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:30.720
And we also split the inner page after we have 1000 inserts in the leaf page.

519
00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:34.100
So it means every 1 million elements we have to do this.

520
00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:35.520
This is really rare.

521
00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:42.580
So this restart here happens extremely rarely and therefore we can afford to do this.

522
00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:44.400
Yes, it should be really rare.

523
00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:46.140
And then we can now we can handle

524
00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:50.420
both fixed size and variable size records.

525
00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:50.820
Fine.

526
00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:55.020
So that's, this covers this case.

527
00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:00.260
Now, there are some more strategies,

528
00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:02.320
I mean, even with log coupling,

529
00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:04.380
I mean, if you do this, then log coupling works.

530
00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.100
The problem with log coupling is,

531
00:38:06.340 --> 00:38:08.160
log coupling is more expensive

532
00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:09.900
than you think at the first glance.

533
00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:11.500
Because what does this mean?

534
00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:14.840
This small picture here.

535
00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:23.540
So if you look at this, we know every node here contains for example 1000 elements to

536
00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:24.180
keep it simple.

537
00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:29.020
That means that if I lock this node here, then I have locked 1000 elements.

538
00:38:30.140 --> 00:38:38.180
If I have locked that node, then I have locked myself and implicitly because nobody can come

539
00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:45.860
by me, I have implicitly locked at least concerning concurrent traversals, my descendants too,

540
00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:49.520
which means that I have locked implicitly 1 million elements.

541
00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:55.180
Because I have 1000 shared nodes, each with 1000 entries, which means by locking this

542
00:38:55.180 --> 00:38:59.900
in a node, I lock at least for a short period of time, 1 million elements.

543
00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:02.600
And so this is pretty expensive.

544
00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:06.780
So, I mean, you have to, in worst case,

545
00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:09.580
is this page that I want to access to this leaf page here,

546
00:39:09.700 --> 00:39:10.660
that's called this L1.

547
00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:14.100
In worst case, the L1 page is not in memory,

548
00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:15.420
so I have to load it from disk,

549
00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:18.020
which means I, let's call this I1.

550
00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:21.400
So I access this I1, I have not locked this one yet,

551
00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:22.520
well, I'll try to lock this.

552
00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:25.440
So I have locked the inner page I1,

553
00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:28.120
so I want to access leaf page L1,

554
00:39:29.180 --> 00:39:31.200
and wait for this leaf page to come,

555
00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:36.960
this time nobody else can access for example L2 because they cannot pass by me.

556
00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:48.300
So I block L1, nobody can access L2 and so I'm blocking implicitly 1000 pages, meaning

557
00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:52.840
one million elements just because I wait for my page L1 and have locked the inner page.

558
00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:58.400
So this is not so, I mean it's correct, but it's not so super great from a performance

559
00:39:58.400 --> 00:39:59.000
perspective.

560
00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:02.560
So performance could be better.

561
00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:06.540
And therefore people said, hey, wouldn't it be great if we don't do this?

562
00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:11.220
So wouldn't it be great if you only link at most one node?

563
00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:15.380
So one node we can lock, but we never lock more than one node.

564
00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:21.920
So this means we lock I1, then I figure out, okay, I want to go to L1, so I know the node,

565
00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:27.560
then I release my lock here and then acquire that lock here instead.

566
00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:31.080
That means I still have to wait until A1 comes from this,

567
00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:33.420
that's true, but in the meantime,

568
00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:35.820
everybody else can go to A1 if they want to.

569
00:40:36.040 --> 00:40:38.260
That's why I don't prevent this,

570
00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:39.760
I get much better concurrency.

571
00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:43.180
So this is the idea of B-linked trees.

572
00:40:43.780 --> 00:40:45.560
So not the whole idea.

573
00:40:45.740 --> 00:40:47.140
So this is the motivation of B-linked trees.

574
00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:48.420
Yeah, so this is what they want.

575
00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:52.200
The problem is, if you do this,

576
00:40:56.480 --> 00:40:59.560
what happens if somebody splits a page behind myself

577
00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:00.120
in the meantime?

578
00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:04.120
Yeah, so I want to get to L1, but I cannot therefore,

579
00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:05.360
because I'm scheduled away,

580
00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:06.380
I don't get to lock whatever.

581
00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:09.540
It could be that the moment I came L1,

582
00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:11.260
my data element, so let's say,

583
00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:13.100
I'm searching for this element X here,

584
00:41:13.860 --> 00:41:14.820
until the point in time

585
00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:16.580
that I finally managed to get to L1,

586
00:41:16.840 --> 00:41:18.040
X has moved somewhere else.

587
00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:19.660
Can happen because somebody split the page.

588
00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:21.400
Yeah, so this means I jumped here

589
00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:22.540
is gone.

590
00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:31.800
And then the idea of be linked trees is to say, okay, I do this, I willingly accept that

591
00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:32.940
my element might be gone.

592
00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:40.800
But because I always have this next pointer here, so if I am searching for a value and

593
00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:44.480
I see the value that I am searching for is larger than the largest value that is on my

594
00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:46.960
current page, I just go to the next page.

595
00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:53.660
So I proactively go to the next page, even though I would not go there, but naturally

596
00:41:53.660 --> 00:41:57.000
I go to the next page and then if x was moved to there, I find it.

597
00:41:57.200 --> 00:42:00.020
Of course, it could be that it was split again, so I have to go to the next page again.

598
00:42:00.180 --> 00:42:06.040
So I follow the pointers here until I either find x or I find elements, values that are

599
00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:09.180
larger than x and then I know for sure that x cannot exist.

600
00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:10.340
So then I can stop.

601
00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:13.000
So this is a so-called B-link idea.

602
00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:23.880
So we go to the neighboring pages and to go there.

603
00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:28.140
By the way you have to do this not only in the leaf pages but also on the inner nodes

604
00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:29.920
because the inner nodes the same can happen.

605
00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:35.180
So I go to an inner node until I arrive there, somebody else has split the page, therefore

606
00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:39.520
I have to introduce the next pointers on inner nodes too.

607
00:42:39.520 --> 00:42:45.080
So, if somebody goes here, doesn't find its value, can go to the next point and so on.

608
00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:55.260
So this is already pretty complicated, but this can be implemented reasonably well as

609
00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:58.140
long as we have only lookup and insert.

610
00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:03.580
What is a total nightmare in this B-Link tree is deletion.

611
00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:10.100
Because with deletion, suddenly values can not only move to the right, but at least in

612
00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:11.360
principle can also move to the left.

613
00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:19.640
So because if I merge them, then they can be balanced with the neighboring nodes, so

614
00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:24.540
it means my values that I'm looking for goes to the left and not only to the right.

615
00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:27.040
And therefore following the next pointer is not good enough.

616
00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:33.560
Now as there are solutions for this, basically this boils down to, you can only merge the

617
00:43:33.580 --> 00:43:38.960
a tree or delete a node if you are sure that at this point in time nobody is currently

618
00:43:39.800 --> 00:43:45.620
going down to this. Yeah, this creates some special programming mechanisms like an epoch

619
00:43:45.620 --> 00:43:54.520
mechanism or something like this. Pretty difficult to implement and, but as I said, many people just

620
00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:59.400
don't bother with this deletion problem, so they don't implement this correctly, then of course

621
00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:04.600
it's easy, but if you want to do proper deletions in B-link trees, this is possible, but quite

622
00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:04.960
challenging.

623
00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:12.480
Now just for completeness, I want to cover one technique more, that is strictly speaking

624
00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:16.300
not on the slides, but nevertheless is pretty interesting.

625
00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:24.000
The problem we have here with synchronization is, with log coupling, is basically a problem

626
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.460
that we have in all kinds of hierarchical data structures.

627
00:44:26.460 --> 00:44:29.960
The problem is that the root here is a bottleneck.

628
00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:37.020
Or in general, the higher up nodes will be visited much more frequently than the lower nodes.

629
00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:41.800
So that means we have many, many logs here, so we make many dots here, many dots here,

630
00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:44.700
a few bit less here, and then even less here.

631
00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:48.620
So this will be extremely hard because everybody goes through the root.

632
00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:51.640
This is of course bad for concurrency.

633
00:44:52.780 --> 00:44:56.980
Interestingly, it's even bad if you have no concordial problem at all.

634
00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.620
So just assume everybody's reading.

635
00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:02.520
So reading is not a problem.

636
00:45:02.700 --> 00:45:08.660
So everybody can get a read log on everything, because I mean, readers don't conflict with

637
00:45:08.660 --> 00:45:09.280
themselves.

638
00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:13.900
So you can have one million people reading the same notes, no problem, conceptually.

639
00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:15.900
But in reality, this is a problem.

640
00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:18.960
So if you acquire many read logs here,

641
00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:27.820
so if you do log reading, log reading, log reading,

642
00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:28.720
and so on, many of these,

643
00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:31.940
this will be a problem in practice,

644
00:45:32.140 --> 00:45:33.500
even though semantically it's not a problem,

645
00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:34.780
but in practice it's a problem.

646
00:45:35.020 --> 00:45:35.420
Why?

647
00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:39.340
Because this log must be implemented somehow.

648
00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:42.280
And the classical way to implement this

649
00:45:42.280 --> 00:45:44.840
is to use a read writer log,

650
00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:48.540
In first approximation, it's a bit more complicated,

651
00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:49.800
but in first approximation,

652
00:45:50.020 --> 00:45:51.300
read-writer logs look like this.

653
00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:56.540
Read-writer log, it means you have two kinds of fields.

654
00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:01.600
You have an exclusive bit here, exclusive bit,

655
00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:03.360
and then you have a counter

656
00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:05.180
that tells you how many readers we have.

657
00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:09.180
Ready, you need probably at least one bit more.

658
00:46:09.960 --> 00:46:13.220
But this is how a very simple read-writer log

659
00:46:13.800 --> 00:46:15.120
could work like.

660
00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:19.060
So you could say, okay, when you try to get,

661
00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:21.060
you lock something in exclusive mode,

662
00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:23.380
you check if this bit here is clear.

663
00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:27.500
If yes, you check if everything is clear.

664
00:46:27.540 --> 00:46:30.080
So if there's no reader and not an exclusive bit,

665
00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.480
and if yes, you set the exclusive bit to one.

666
00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:34.260
And of course you do this using atomics

667
00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:36.760
or some other mechanisms that is thread safe.

668
00:46:37.100 --> 00:46:38.440
And so you set this to one.

669
00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:40.640
And of course, if you cannot set this,

670
00:46:40.720 --> 00:46:42.080
then you have to wait until this changes.

671
00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:46.520
And if you read this, the end you increase the read counter.

672
00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:48.240
So this you increase the reader here.

673
00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:50.360
You basically plus plus on the reader.

674
00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:55.280
And then nobody will create an exclusive log as long as there are readers.

675
00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:58.720
So this is classical implementation read writer log.

676
00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:00.820
And this is of course, semantically correct.

677
00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:06.320
But in practice, it has very poor performance for this because this log that we have here

678
00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:10.700
at the root of this node is changed all the time.

679
00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:18.200
changing and then many threads try to change the same memory location this is slow.

680
00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:23.080
Now because on the practical level the cache lines that holds this read writer lock here

681
00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:28.760
will bounce between the different cores because everybody will try to update this counter

682
00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:30.500
all the time this is very slow.

683
00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:35.920
This is a performance problem even without contention.

684
00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:46.420
So, therefore, there is an alternative approach that allows you to solve both this contention

685
00:47:46.420 --> 00:47:53.960
problem in the log and the concordial problem in concerning other readers as well as log

686
00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:54.280
coupling.

687
00:47:54.600 --> 00:47:57.540
And this is something that is called optimistic log coupling.

688
00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:01.480
So, how does this work?

689
00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:06.240
Optimistic lock-up link.

690
00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:20.880
And basically the key idea is that we assume that if a system is fast, conflicts are unlikely.

691
00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:24.860
Of course conflicts can happen, but most of the time we have no conflicts.

692
00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:29.260
Therefore we optimize for the case that we have no conflict and we do it as follows.

693
00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:35.760
